{"version":"2026.10.11-r25","id":"Q25102255","formula":"C₁₆H₁₇NO₄","hill":"C16H17NO4","elements":["H","C","N","O"],"composition":{"C":16.0,"H":17.0,"N":1.0,"O":4.0},"names":{"en":"Noroxymorphone"},"descriptions":{"en":"Noroxymorphone is an opioid which is both a metabolite of oxymorphone and oxycodone and is manufactured specifically as an intermediate in the production of opioid antagonists such as naltrexone and others. It is a potent agonist of the μ-opioid receptor, but is poorly able to cross the blood–brain barrier into the central nervous system, and for this reason, has only minimal analgesic activity. In the United States, noroxymorphone is controlled as a Schedule II Narcotic controlled substance with an ACSCN of 9637."},"fame":1,"pubchemCid":5497189,"mass":287.31,"charge":0,"smiles":"C1C[C@]2([C@H]3CC4=C5[C@@]2(CCN3)[C@H](C1=O)OC5=C(C=C4)O)O","inchikey":"HLMSIZPQBSYUNL-IPOQPSJVSA-N","iupac":"(4R,4aS,7aR,12bS)-4a,9-dihydroxy-1,2,3,4,5,6,7a,13-octahydro-4,12-methanobenzofuro[3,2-e]isoquinolin-7-one","classes":[{"qid":"Q113145171","names":{"en":"type of chemical entity","ru":"тип химического объекта","uk":"тип хімічної сутності"}}],"structureInfo":{"kind":"molecule","source":"pubchem","ref":"5497189","atoms":38,"license":"PubChem (NIH), public data"},"structure":{"kind":"molecule","atoms":[{"x":1.52,"y":1.143,"z":1.23,"el":"O"},{"x":-2.944,"y":0.981,"z":0.261,"el":"O"},{"x":1.166,"y":3.277,"z":-0.394,"el":"O"},{"x":4.055,"y":-0.137,"z":0.541,"el":"O"},{"x":-2.721,"y":-1.797,"z":0.513,"el":"N"},{"x":-0.661,"y":0.268,"z":0.657,"el":"C"},{"x":-1.812,"y":0.348,"z":-0.355,"el":"C"},{"x":-2.19,"y":-1.125,"z":-0.7,"el":"C"},{"x":0.142,"y":1.547,"z":0.946,"el":"C"},{"x":-1.154,"y":-0.434,"z":1.945,"el":"C"},{"x":0.462,"y":-0.521,"z":0.076,"el":"C"},{"x":-1.448,"y":1.209,"z":-1.577,"el":"C"},{"x":-1.016,"y":-1.943,"z":-1.331,"el":"C"},{"x":-1.789,"y":-1.802,"z":1.645,"el":"C"},{"x":0.348,"y":-1.586,"z":-0.799,"el":"C"},{"x":-1.0,"y":2.604,"z":-1.143,"el":"C"},{"x":0.